{
  "id": "cdb0de59-5015-4fa6-986c-4cd1527dd2b3",
  "label": "More and less in a perfection: primitive comparative versus represented-degree comparative",
  "term": "more and less (magis et minus) in a perfection",
  "sense_definition_ids": [
    "5766de66-8a37-5b04-aa65-9eec53319f50",
    "37ffc062-8966-4491-b6bc-4bba1b163f98"
  ],
  "resolution": "MORE_1 is the primitive comparative of the quarta via, MoreIn r x y (DEF-MORE-IN): x is more r than y, with no order law, measure or comparability imposed; it is the comparative every grades, maximum and cause-of-perfection premise of the case uses, and its whole-binder tags name the declarations that state those premises over it. MORE_2 (DegreeComparative) is the comparative represented by a preorder-valued degree, of which both the homogeneous-scale reading (degree = the order on beings, HomogeneousScaleReading) and the approach-to-primary reading (degree = near, ApproachToPrimaryReading) are instances at a fixed respect; it is neutral between the univocal and the analogical reading. It is a strict order in every respect, while MORE_1 is not constrained to be one (Grades.primitive_not_degreeComparative). Arguments pass between the senses only through the explicit open calibration premises MoreInExceedsDegree and DegreeExcessAmongBearersIsMoreIn (Round3FourthWay), which relate MoreIn to a represented degree; no statement identifies the two. Defining MoreIn as MORE_2 would impose irreflexivity and transitivity, and would conceal the calibration premises. Follow-up: once the Round3 calibration statements link the MORE_2 definition, the arguments using them should list them as sense_bridges. DISAMB-ORDER-OF-BEING records the order models at esse and DISAMB-BEING-UNIVOCITY which premises survive on which model. The recorded senses are not claimed to be exhaustive or exclusive.",
